I was able to find the source of the problem and fix it. The memory leak is due to torch.load with weights_only = False. Since setting this parameter to True results in an error when loading a PyG.Data object, I had to store the tensors as dictionary entries as seen below.

```
def get(self, idx):
    path = os.path.join(self.data_dir, f'{idx}.pt')
    if os.path.exists(path):
        data_dict = torch.load(path)
        return torch_geometric.data.Data(**data_dict)
    else:
        x = torch.randn((500, 1024))
        edge_index = torch.randint(0, 500, (2, 1500))
        y = torch.tensor([idx % 2], dtype = torch.long)
        data = dict(x = x, edge_index = edge_index, y = y)
        torch.save(data, path)
        return torch_geometric.data.Data(**data)
```

Hope this helps someone facing a similar problem! :)
